course: world
lesson: 190
title: Saying "I was wrong" and laughing without being cruel
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Everybody gets things wrong. The difference between a good thinker and a stuck one is what happens next: "OK, I was wrong, that's actually cool" or "Nah, that's fake."
explain: Oscar argued for ten minutes that a whale is a fish. Then the teacher showed him: whales breathe air and feed their babies milk, so they are mammals. Oscar: "OK. I was wrong." His friend: "Respect. Rare W."
explain: Laughing is allowed, and good laughing is aimed at the idea, never at the person: "Cows drink milk? Ha, grown-up cows drink water, calves drink milk" helps; "Haha, you're so dumb" just makes someone go quiet.
explain: The best laugh of all is at your own mistake. Mia: "I said all dogs are brown, then my own dog walked in. He's white." Everyone laughs, including her.
explain: And no joke is worth danger: "Don't believe in gravity? Jump off the roof!" goes too far. Drop a pencil instead; it proves the point just fine.
fact: Whales breathe air, feed their babies milk and are mammals, not fish.
fact: Changing your mind when the evidence is clear is how you learn; it makes you look smart, not silly.
fact: Saying "I was wrong" quickly saves time and grumpiness, and then you can fix the problem, like going to another shop.
fact: Grown-up cows drink water; calves drink milk from their mothers.
fact: Laugh at the idea, not the person: mocking a person makes them less likely to learn.
fact: A tablespoon holds about three teaspoons.
fact: Bats aren't blind: many see quite well, and many also use echoes to find their way in the dark.
fact: Calling clear evidence "fake" just to avoid being wrong is called motivated reasoning.
fact: In science, tomatoes are fruits because they grow from the flower and hold the seeds; in cooking they are used like vegetables.
fact: Gravity can be shown safely by dropping a pencil; never dare anyone to do something dangerous to win an argument.
fact: Laughing at someone for a long time makes them scared to try; laugh briefly with them, then help.
fact: Smart people of all ages change their minds when they see new evidence.
fact: Laughing at your own mistakes makes them easier to admit.
fact: Words people use: "Rare W" means a rare win, something to respect; "character development" means getting better, like a hero in a story; "my bad" means "my mistake"; "absolute weapon" (UK, jokey) means a legend; "iconic" means unforgettable; "cwtch" (Wales) means a cuddle; "tamping" (Wales) means furious.
exercise: Oscar argued that a whale is a fish, then said, "OK. I was wrong. That's actually cool." What did he do well? | Changed his mind when the evidence was clear | Kept arguing until everyone else just gave up | Pretended he had never said it | Blamed the teacher for confusing him
exercise: Why are whales mammals? | They breathe air and feed their babies milk | They live in water all their lives and have fins | They are huge and swim faster than any other fish | They eat fish and tiny sea creatures called krill
exercise: Dad insisted the shop was open on Sunday. It's closed. Dad: "Well, it SHOULD be open." What would be the better answer? | "I was wrong. Let's go somewhere else." | "The shop is wrong, not me." | "Let's wait outside until it opens." | "Shops aren't allowed to close on Sundays."
exercise: Ella laughs at her friend: "Haha, cows drink milk, you're so dumb." Her friend goes quiet. What went wrong? | She mocked the person instead of the idea | She explained the facts to her friend too kindly | She only laughed at the idea | Nothing; laughing always helps
exercise: What do grown-up cows drink? | Water | Milk | Juice | Nothing at all
exercise: Kai used a tablespoon of salt instead of a teaspoon. About how many teaspoons are in a tablespoon? | 3 | 2 | 10 | 1
exercise: Everyone laughs at Kai's salty cake, and Kai laughs too: "OK, so that's not a teaspoon." Is this cruel? | No; they laugh together and he joins in | Yes; any laughing at a mistake is cruel | Yes; only Kai is allowed to laugh | No, because nobody likes cake anyway
exercise: Leo told everyone bats are blind. Shown a video of fruit bats seeing well: "Nah, that's fake." What is he doing? | Refusing evidence so he doesn't have to be wrong | Checking the evidence carefully | Changing his mind fairly | Laughing at his own mistake
exercise: What is true about bats? | Many see quite well, and many also use echoes | All bats are completely blind from the day they're born | Bats can only see in daylight | Bats can only see red light
exercise: Ruby said tomatoes are vegetables. The teacher explains they're fruits in science. Ruby: "Thanks, I didn't know that." Why are tomatoes fruits in science? | They grow from the flower and hold the seeds | They are red and round | They grow on tall trees | They taste sweet like apples
exercise: Freddie's friend says, "I don't believe in gravity." Freddie: "Prove it, jump off the roof." What is wrong? | The comeback could get a person badly hurt | It is a fair test that anyone should try once | Gravity doesn't work on roofs | Nothing; it's only a joke
exercise: How can you show gravity safely? | Drop a pencil | Jump off something high | Let go from the top of a tree | Throw a friend into the air
exercise: Jade says something wrong; two boys whisper "dumb" and snigger for the rest of the lesson. What does this do? | It makes her scared to try | It helps her learn faster | It makes the lesson better for all | It doesn't affect anyone
exercise: Oscar's grandad: "I was wrong about that. Your phone does help me call my sister." What does this show? | People of any age can change their minds | Old people can't use phones | Grandad lost the argument badly and gave up | Phones only work for young people
exercise: Mia: "I said all dogs are brown, then my own dog walked in. He's white." Why is her joke a good one? | She laughs at her own reasoning, not at a person | She laughs at her dog's colour | She makes fun of brown dogs | She pretends she was right all along
exercise: What was enough to break "all dogs are brown"? | One white dog | A survey of every dog | A photo of a brown dog | A friend's opinion
test: Oscar's friend: "Bro just admitted he was wrong. Respect, honestly. Rare W." Why the respect? | Admitting a mistake when shown evidence is smart | Being wrong is always cool | Whales really are fish | He won the argument
test: Mum: "It should be open and it isn't, Dave. Like your mind." Dad: "...Fair. I was wrong. Let's get chips." What made it end well? | Dad admitted it and moved on to a fix | Mum stopped talking to him | The shop opened after all | Dad proved the shop was wrong to close
test: Ella's older sister: "Laugh at the idea, not the person. Calves drink milk; grown-up cows drink water. Now she knows." What did the sister do? | Turned mocking into helping | Mocked the friend even harder | Told Ella never to laugh again | Agreed that cows drink milk
test: Mum: "That's the saltiest cake in history. Absolute weapon, Kai. Teaspoon's the small one, love." How can you tell this is friendly? | Kai laughs too, and Mum tells him which spoon is right | Mum shouts at him in front of everyone | Nobody explains the mistake to him | Kai leaves the room upset
test: Leo's friend: "It's from a nature documentary, Leo. Just say 'my bad' and move on." What should Leo do? | Admit bats aren't blind and move on | Say every documentary on TV is totally fake | Look for a video of a blind bat online | Stop talking to his friend for a whole week
test: Ruby's friend: "Look at Ruby, learning stuff. Character development." What did Ruby do? | Said "thanks, I didn't know that" | Said tomatoes are vegetables again | Argued with the teacher | Ignored the teacher
test: What would have happened if Freddie's friend had really taken the roof dare? | He could have been badly hurt | He would have floated down safely | He would have proved gravity is fake | Nothing, because roofs are low
test: The boys' mate: "Bro, last week you said Australia was in Europe. Calm down." What is he reminding them? | Everyone gets things wrong sometimes | Australia really is in Europe, near Austria | Jade is always wrong | Laughing at people is fine
test: Oscar: "Grandad's character development is insane. Next he'll learn emojis." Is this cruel? | No; it's a playful joke that praises him | Yes; he says Grandad is insane, which is rude | Yes; emojis are rude | No, because Grandad can't hear him
test: Mia's friend: "Your dog came in to personally disprove you. Iconic." Why does everyone laugh, Mia included? | She joked about her own mistake | They are all laughing at her silly dog | They think Mia is silly | Her dog turned out to be brown
test: Welsh nan in Cardiff: "Tamping I was when I got it wrong, love, but come here for a cwtch, we all get things wrong." What is Nan's message? | Mistakes happen to everyone, so be kind about them | Getting things wrong makes you a bad person | Only children ever make mistakes | Never admit a mistake to anyone
test: What would happen in a class where everyone was laughed at for every mistake? | People would stop trying and stop asking questions | Everyone would learn much faster | Nobody would ever make a mistake again | The class would be more fun for everyone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of a pupil who abandons a mistaken belief upon being shown clear evidence? | It is a sign of sound reasoning | It is a sign of weakness of character | It is contrary to the practice of scientists | It is permissible only for adults
exam: Identify the classification of whales, having regard to the facts that they breathe air and nourish their young with milk. | Mammals | Fish | Reptiles | Amphibians
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the beverage consumed by adult cattle. | Water | Milk | Fruit juice | No beverage whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the principal objection to the remark "Prove it, jump off the roof" when addressed to a person who doubts gravity. | It invites conduct that may result in serious injury | It fails to mention the speed of falling | It is insufficiently amusing | It concerns gravity rather than the roof
